        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "# DyPE-M for MOSS-Image\n\n**This workflow is tuned.** `target_scale = 1.25` + `isotropic_scale = True` gives correct\nanatomy at 8 MP and beats no-DyPE at 9.4 MP on 1:1 crops.\n\n## The two settings that matter\n\n**`target_scale` (1.25)** - pins the extrapolation strength so it does not change with\noutput size. Lower = milder = less object inflation. 1.2-1.3 is the sweet spot.\n`0` falls back to `base_resolution`.\n\n**`isotropic_scale` (True)** - derives ONE scale from the pixel area and applies it to both\naxes. **Leave this on.** With it off, the axes are scaled independently, so they disagree\nabout the canvas size by exactly the aspect ratio: a 25% horizontal stretch at 3200x2560,\n78% at 4096x2304. That was the cause of elongated limbs, warped arches and the\n\"funhouse mirror\" look.\n\n> **`base_resolution` only matters when `target_scale = 0`.** While target_scale is non-zero\n> it is ignored, and the console prints a line saying so. base 2048 at 2560x2560 equals\n> target_scale 1.25, which is where that default came from.\n\n## Other knobs\n\n| widget | what it does |\n|---|---|\n| `enabled` | A/B toggle. False = byte-identical to no patch. |\n| `method` | `yarn` = paper's branch (most detail). `uniform` = milder. `ntk` = smoothest. |\n| `schedule` | `adaptive` (default) = analytic magnitude x measured h/w ratio. `hybrid` = pure analytic. (Old name `aniso` still works.) |\n| `lambda_s` | 0 = off, 1 = paper strength. |\n| `start_percent` | `0.125` skips step 0, which commits the layout. |\n| `base_resolution` | Only used when `target_scale = 0`. |\n\n## Measured object extent (sphere, 8.19 MP, 3200x2560; native = 0.755)\n\n| setting | axis ratio | extent |\n|---|---|---|\n| target 1.25, isotropic | **1.015** | 0.642 |\n| base 1024, isotropic | 1.091 | 0.775 |\n| base 2048, per-axis | **1.200** | 0.739 |\n\nSquare canvases are unaffected by the isotropic change - bit-identical output.\n"
      ]
    }
